## 🔹 IAM and Secrets Manager

One of the most important concepts I learned is that **AWS Secrets Manager works closely with IAM**.

We can use IAM policies to control which users, roles, or AWS services are allowed to access specific secrets.

For example:

```text
Application
    ↓
IAM Role
    ↓
IAM Policy
    ↓
AWS Secrets Manager
    ↓
production/database
```

A policy can allow an application to perform:

```text
secretsmanager:GetSecretValue
```

on a specific secret.

This follows the principle of **least privilege**, where an application receives only the permissions it actually needs.

---

## 🔹 Secret Encryption

AWS Secrets Manager encrypts secrets at rest using **AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)**.

The secret can be encrypted using an AWS-managed key or an appropriate customer-managed KMS key depending on the requirements.

Conceptually:

```text
Secret
   ↓
Encryption
   ↓
AWS KMS
   ↓
Encrypted Secret
```

When an authorized application retrieves the secret, AWS handles the decryption process according to the configured permissions.

---

## 🔹 Secret Rotation

Another important feature I learned is **secret rotation**.

Instead of keeping the same password forever, credentials can be periodically changed.

For example:

```text
Old Password
     ↓
Rotation
     ↓
New Password
```

This reduces the risk associated with long-lived credentials.

AWS Secrets Manager supports automated rotation for supported use cases, including certain database credentials.

---

## 🔹 Versioning

Secrets Manager can maintain different versions of a secret.

For example:

```text
Secret
│
├── Version 1 → Old credentials
└── Version 2 → New credentials
```

This helps manage changes to secrets and supports rotation workflows.

---

## 🔹 Secrets Manager with EC2

An EC2-based application can retrieve secrets from Secrets Manager using an **IAM role attached to the EC2 instance**.

The flow is:

```text
EC2 Instance
     ↓
IAM Role
     ↓
IAM Policy
     ↓
AWS Secrets Manager
     ↓
Secret
```

This is preferable to storing long-lived AWS access keys inside the EC2 instance.

---

## 🔹 Secrets Manager with Lambda

Lambda functions can also retrieve secrets from Secrets Manager.

For example:

```text
Lambda Function
      ↓
Execution Role
      ↓
IAM Permission
      ↓
AWS Secrets Manager
      ↓
Database Credentials
```

The Lambda execution role can be granted permission to retrieve only the required secret.

---

## 🔹 Secrets Manager with EKS

I also connected Secrets Manager with my EKS learning.

A Kubernetes application may need a database password or API key.

Instead of putting the secret directly inside a Kubernetes manifest, we can use AWS Secrets Manager as the external secret store.

A common architecture is:

```text
AWS Secrets Manager
        ↓
IAM / EKS Pod Identity or IRSA
        ↓
Kubernetes Workload
        ↓
Pod
        ↓
Application
```

For example, an EKS application could be allowed to retrieve:

```text
production/database
```

without giving the Pod access to unrelated secrets.

This is where **IAM, Service Accounts, OIDC/IRSA, or EKS Pod Identity** become important for securely giving workloads access to AWS resources.

